    \(0.973\)
  \end{enumerate}

  \textbf{Solution}\\
  Primeiramente devemos completar a tabela com as probabilidades
  marginais de \(X\) e \(Y\):

  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}cccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  X Y & 1 & 2 & P(X=x) \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  3 & 0.07 & 0.05 & 0.12 \\
  4 & 0.01 & 0.05 & 0.06 \\
  5 & 0.39 & 0.43 & 0.82 \\
  P(Y=y) & 0.47 & 0.53 & 1 \\
  \end{longtable}

  Em seguida, calculamos \[\begin{aligned}
  \text{Var}(X) &=& E\left(X^2\right)-[E(X)]^2 = 22.54-(4.7)^2 = 0.45\\
  \text{Var}(Y) &=& E\left(Y^2\right)-[E(Y)]^2 = 2.59-(1.53)^2 = 0.249
  \end{aligned}\]

  A distribuição do produto é dada por

  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}ccccccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  k & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 8 & 10 \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  P(XY = k) & 0.07 & 0.01 & 0.39 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.43 \\
  \end{longtable}

  De modo que
  \[E(XY) = 3\times0.07+4\times0.01+5\times0.39+6\times0.05+8\times0.05+10\times0.43 = 7.2\]
  e, portanto,
  \[\text{Corr}(X,Y) = \frac{\text{Cov}(X,Y)}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(X)}\sqrt{\text{Var}(Y)}} = \frac{E(XY)-E(X)E(Y)}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(X)}\sqrt{\text{Var}(Y)}} = 0.027\]

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumii{(\alph{enumii})}
  \item
    Falso
  \item
    Verdadeiro
  \item
    Falso
  \item
